                <pb facs="0563" n="449" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg449"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 Arms Truce/21: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-19T14:00:00-04:00">September 19, 1931—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>114. Reference is made to your telegram of September 18, midnight, No. 139. When
                    the official invitation has been received by you you may reply to it by a note
                    transmitted to the Secretariat of the League on my behalf, in which you accept
                    in appropriate language the League’s invitation to the United States to send a
                    representative to the Third Committee of the Assembly for the sole purpose of
                    participating in the discussions of the proposal of Signor Grandi for a truce on
                    armaments (as described in your telegram No. 132, September 16), and informing
                    the Secretariat that you have been appointed the representative of the United
                    States. You are directed to telegraph the Department immediately the text of the
                    League’s invitation and of your reply thereto, which should not be presented to
                    the Secretariat before Monday morning. On Monday morning we will also give this
                    to the press.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
